                        <forename>Godfrey</forename>
                        <surname>Higgins</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1773">
                        <placeName/>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1833-08-09"/>
                    <occupation>historian of religion</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Higgins resigned an army commission in 1813 and dedicated himself to scholarship. Among his publications were <title>Horae sabbaticae</title> (1826), and 
      <title>The Celtic Druids</title> (1829) and <title>Anacalypsis: an attempt to draw aside the veil of the Saitic Isis, or, An inquiry into the origin of languages, nations, and religions</title>, 
      (1836). </p>
                        <p>The identification can be confirmed by the entry where Godwin takes tea at <persName key="FRE01">William Frend's</persName> on 27 February 1795. Frend was a 
    close acquaintance of Higgins whom he met at Cambridge. </p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="other">
                        <title>DNB</title>
                    </bibl>
                </person>
